<h2>Guidance for the reader</h2>
<h3>What this is</h3>
<p>The difference between an approved baseline and a later capture of the same subject, as one deterministic document. The same two bundles always produce the same bytes and the same diff_digest.</p>
<h3>What it answers</h3>
<p>What changed, and at which severity the policy named in this document rated each change. Entries marked coverage_increased are the opposite of drift: there the later capture could see more than the baseline could, so the difference is in the question, not in the host.</p>
<h3>What it does not answer</h3>
<p>Why something changed, and whether the change was authorised. It also does not re-verify the two bundles: both were verified when this diff was produced, and a diff is never produced over a baseline that failed verification.</p>
<h3>What to do next</h3>
<p>Read the entries at and above the severity your process cares about, decide whether the change was intended, and then either act on the host or approve the new state as the new baseline. Approving is a deliberate step: there is no automatic baseline update.</p>
<pre>skillctl trust-freeze diff --baseline &lt;baseline&gt; --current &lt;capture&gt; --trusted-key &lt;public key&gt;</pre>
<h3>To reproduce this document from the two bundles it names:</h3>
<pre>skillctl trust-freeze diff --baseline &lt;baseline&gt; --current &lt;capture&gt; --trusted-key &lt;public key&gt;</pre>
<p class="note">Two runs of that command over unchanged bundles give the same diff_digest as this page. A different digest means a different input, not a different mood.</p>



